<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hernando Pizarro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1501–<a href="/years/1578">1578</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hernando Pizarro was a Spanish conquistador, the only legitimate brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>, and a leader in the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. He held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a>'s siege of 1536–1537, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-salinas">Battle of Las Salinas</a> in 1538 and had him executed. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> he was imprisoned for some twenty years over Almagro's death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro.jpg" alt="Hernando Pizarro" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fondo Antiguo de la Biblioteca de la Universidad de Sevilla from Sevilla, España · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historia_de_la_conquista_del_Per%C3%BA,_1851_%22Hernando_Pizarro_herido_en_Pun%C3%A1%22._(3971678154).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hernando was the only one of the Pizarro brothers born in wedlock; Francisco, Juan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a> were his half-brothers, and through his father he was a second cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>. He was educated and gained influence at the Spanish court, and in 1530 he sailed for the New World with Francisco and accompanied him in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In November 1533 Hernando wrote a letter describing the expedition, in which he praised the paved roads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> mountains and their bridges of stone, wood and network. That year he was sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> with the royal fifth for the emperor, taken from the treasure collected for Atahuallpa's ransom. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> on 9 January 1534 and met Charles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a>, who confirmed Francisco's grants and extended them seventy leagues further south, while giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> a grant two hundred leagues beyond.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, Hernando ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> with Juan and Gonzalo, governing with an iron fist and helping to suppress the uprisings led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a>. Returning from a fruitless expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, Almagro claimed Cuzco as his share, seized it in 1537 and captured Hernando and Juan. Released after negotiations between Almagro and Francisco, Hernando came back with Gonzalo and an army in 1538 and won a decisive victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-salinas">Battle of Las Salinas</a>, capturing Almagro and the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Almagro's execution later that year and the disorder of the Spanish infighting caused an outcry at court. Hernando returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1539 to lobby for his brothers, but despite his bribes he was held in the Castle of La Mota from June 1541 until May 1561. In 1552 he married his niece Francisca Pizarro Yupanqui, the 17-year-old daughter of Francisco and Inés Yupanqui, and they had five children; the descendants of their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> y Pizarro were the Marqueses de La Conquista. Hernando spent his last years in his palace at Trujillo, where he died in 1578.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hernando Pizarro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New Castile was governed by Pizarro with his brothers <b>Hernando</b>, Gonzalo and Juan.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-castile">Governorate of New Castile</a></li><li><blockquote>Returning from Chile in 1537, he seized Cusco from <b>Hernando</b> and Gonzalo Pizarro during the rebellion of Manco Inca.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1533 <b>Hernando Pizarro</b> looted the temple.</blockquote><a href="/state/ychsma">Ychsma</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Trujillo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Trujillo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-5.88&amp;year=1551" aria-label="Trujillo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4405 750 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hernando Pizarro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4855 1020h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4855 1020h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-5.88&amp;year=1551">What was here in 1551? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
